# <Library short name> commit message template.
#
# Lines beginning with '#' are stripped from the final commit message.
#
# Conventional-commit prefix examples (from root scicomp-research-skills
# AGENTS.md Section 6.3):
#   feat: <new feature / new public API>
#   fix:  <bug fix / numerical-correctness fix>
#   docs: <documentation change>
#   chore: <housekeeping; CI / dependency bumps>
#   refactor: <reorg without behaviour change>
#   test: <test additions / changes>
#   perf: <performance improvement>
#
# After the subject line + blank line, write the body explaining WHY
# the change was made (the diff already shows WHAT). Reference issues
# / PRs / journal entries / impl-notes that motivated the change. For
# numerical-correctness fixes, cite the source of the corrected
# expected value (analytical / MMS / reference impl / benchmark /
# invariant / asymptotic) per
# research-software-engineering/references/01-numerical-correctness.md.
#
# The Co-Authored-By trailer below records substantive AI assistance
# per the framework's default (root AGENTS.md Section 6.3 "AI
# co-authorship attribution (default = ON)"). Delete the trailer for
# human-only commits. Per-project AGENTS.md "Project-specific
# overrides" may flip the default if your project has a reason to
# omit trailers; if so, delete this template line + the trailer below.
#
# To use this template, run once after bootstrap:
#   git config --local commit.template .gitmessage
#
# More on git trailers: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-interpret-trailers



Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
